15, 16. The society of the wicked (way or
path) is dangerous. Avoid the beginnings of sin (Pr 4:14;
Ps 1:1; 119:101).

paths of uprightness—or,
"plainness."

walk—habitually act;

(Compare Pr 4:25). Avoid all by-paths of evil (De 2:27;
17:11). A life of integrity
requires attention to heart, speech, eyes, and conduct.

Fools not only fail to benefit others, as
do the righteous, but procure their own ruin (compare Pr 10:11, 17;
Ho 4:6).

(Compare Pr 12:3; Ps 37:9-11;
102:28).

earth—or, "land of promise."

trouble—agitation, implying the
anxieties and perplexities attending wealth held by worldlings (Pr 16:18;
1Ti 6:6).

(Compare Pr 6:14; 10:31).

whisperer—prater, talebearer (Pr 18:8;
26:20).

One so corrupt as to take a bribe evinces
his high estimate of it by subjection to its influence (Pr 18:16;
19:6).

Though wealth cannot buy wisdom for those
who do not love it, yet wisdom procures wealth (Pr 3:16;
14:24).

that his heart … itself—that
is, takes pleasure in revealing his folly (Pr 12:23;
15:2).

30, 31. Men's best devices and reliances are
vain compared with God's, or without His aid (Pr 19:21;
Ps 20:7; 33:17).

humility and the fear of the Lord—are
in apposition; one produces the other. On the results, compare Pr 3:16;
8:18.

The influence of wealth sets aside moral
distinctions is implied, and, of course, disapproved (compare Pr 19:6;
21:14, &c.).

